A host's keyed packages evaluate each module once, not once an extension.

A keyed package's extensions already share one factory each across
folders, but jiti gives each extension's import a module cache of its
own, so a library two of its extensions import is evaluated once for
each. A keyed package's loads now share one module cache, the package's
own, so each file they reach is evaluated once for the host. Node's module
cache is neither read nor written, and every other package's loads keep
their modules their own. A reload, and a link moving off a copy, give the
package's next load a new cache, and its loads take turns, so none is
given a module another is still evaluating.

